    "definition": {
      "title": "Numeral Triangles",
      "statement": "## Standard input and output\n\nEach run handles **one** case. Read from standard input and print the result to standard output. Do not read a case count or print case numbers, prompts, or labels. Use the line structure shown in the test-case input below.\n\n### Problem\n\nConstruct a Numeral Octal Triangle according to the following rules. You will be given three positive integers: s, a starting number; d, a delta (the amount by which to increase each number in the triangle); and r the number of rows. The numbers s and d will be in octal.\n\n1. The first row contains the number s.\n\n2. Each of the next rows has one more number than the previous row.\n\n3. Each number in the triangle is d more than the previous number in the triangle.\n\nHere are two examples of Numeral Octal Triangles:\n\nstart=2, delta=3, rows=5\n\n{{diagram:p-929ad08d2bcc25c9}}\n\nstart=221, delta=2, rows=4\n\n{{diagram:p-3fc4cbf4947558e4}}\n\n### Input\n\nThe input has 3 positive integers, s, d, and r. The numbers are separated by spaces and each is less than $1{,}000{,}000_8$. Recall that $s$ and $d$ are in octal.\n\n### Output\n\nPrint the sum of all of the digits on the rth row of the Numeral Octal Triangle as a base 10 number. For example, the output for the above table on the left is: 4 + 0 + 4 + 3 + 4 + 6 + 5 + 1 + 5 + 4 = 36.\n",
      "mode": "stdio",
      "comparator": "tokens",
      "timeLimitMs": 2000,
      "memoryLimitMb": 256,
      "starterCode": "# Read one case from standard input.\n# Print the result to standard output.\n",
